<TopicPage slug="subjects/masonry/foundational/masonry-safety-and-tools">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Masonry is the craft of building with small units — brick, block, stone — glued by mortar into
    walls that last centuries. Its foundation lesson is the tool kit and the safety discipline,
    because the work is heavy, sharp and done above the ground.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mason's kit, from trowel to scaffold" widget="equationStep" config={toolsCfg} />

  <h2>Silica: the modern mason's occupational disease</h2>
  <p>
    Cutting brick, block and stone releases crystalline silica dust — small enough to reach deep
    into lungs, and the cause of silicosis, an incurable scarring disease. Modern regulation
    (OSHA's silica standard) requires wet cutting or vacuum extraction and respirators. The trade's
    newest safety battles are fought in microns.
  </p>

  <h2>Heavy things, repeated</h2>
  <p>
    A brick weighs about 3–4 kg; a mason places hundreds a day. Material handling is therefore a
    safety system of its own — staging at waist height, lifting with legs, scaffolds rated for
    pallets of units plus mortar boards. The trade's chronic injuries are predictable, which is
    exactly why they are preventable.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the tools look ancient</h2>
  <p>
    The trowel and level would be recognised by a mason from two thousand years ago; the grinder
    and scaffold system would not. Masonry is a craft where hand skill still decides quality —
    which is why the apprenticeship (typically three to four years) spends most of its time with
    mortar on the hands, not in a book.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCCER Masonry curriculum; OSHA silica standard 29 CFR 1926.1153; Kreh, <em>Masonry Skills</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
